      "text": "STRATEGY :\n\nreturn the k closest points to\nthe origin (0, 0).\n\n1. This is heap problem \n2. Which heap to use \n    MinHeap \n    Max heap \n    Answer -> as we need the \n    closest , i think MaxHeap\n    is the correct choice \n3. Distance formula is given by\nformula\n \nMath.sqrt((x1 - x2)**2 + (y1-\ny2)**2)\n\n4. we want the minimum distance\nminHeap , let's initialize the\nminHeap\n\n5. we want the top k closest\nelements , but we can store\ndistances , but how we can\nstore \n\n6. i think first calcualte all\ndistances and push to the\nminHeap and map is also needed\nto store point\n\n7. but to store that points we\ncan use map , i think ",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "STRATEGY :\n\nreturn the k closest points to the origin (0, 0).\n\n1. This is heap problem \n2. Which heap to use \n    MinHeap \n    Max heap \n    Answer -> as we need the \n    closest , i think MaxHeap\n    is the correct choice \n3. Distance formula is given by\nformula\n \nMath.sqrt((x1 - x2)**2 + (y1-y2)**2)\n\n4. we want the minimum distance\nminHeap , let's initialize the minHeap\n\n5. we want the top k closest elements , but we can store distances , but how we can store \n\n6. i think first calcualte all distances and push to the minHeap and map is also needed to store point\n\n7. but to store that points we can use map , i think ",
